    "result_text": "There's only the one test class, and it passes. The full build succeeds.\n\n## Summary\n\nImplemented `membershipNumber` formatted `<customerCode>-M<YY>` (YY = last two digits of the registration date year, e.g. `SMI-0007-M26`).\n\nI treated it as a **derived, read-only field** \u2014 exactly like the existing `displayName`, `initials`, and `householdId` fields \u2014 because the test confirms it's \"derived from the owner's own fields, so seed-independent.\" It's computed at serialization time rather than stored, so no entity/persistence changes were needed.\n\nTwo files touched, each with a small, self-contained edit:\n\n1. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added the `membershipNumber` read-only property to the `Owner` schema (so the generated `OwnerDto` carries it).\n2. **`OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 added one `@Mapping` line computing it via a MapStruct expression:\n   ```java\n   owner.getCustomerCode() + \"-M\" + String.format(\"%02d\", owner.getRegistrationDate().getYear() % 100)\n   ```\n\nKeeping structural impact low: I avoided touching the `Owner` entity (large class, high WMC_other), the POST pipeline YAML, and avoided adding a new function class to the creation flow. The mapper change is a single annotation on a bodyless interface method (surrounding methods are trivial CC 1). `registrationDate` is guaranteed non-null (`BuildOwner` defaults it to `now()`), so the expression can't NPE. Since GET and POST both serialize through `toOwnerDto`, the field appears consistently on both create and fetch responses. Full test suite is green.",
